Forked at tag v0.1.5 (commit 24a3517e1e97670daae1ab3e76cb4b2bc48cbd17) into
core/wakeword/src/main/kotlin. Local patches (see the "VoxCommander patch" markers in each file):
- audio/AudioRecorder.kt: an RMS silence gate that drops buffers below an energy floor before they
  are ever converted or emitted, so WakeWordEngine's ONNX inference (mel-spectrogram + embedding +
  classifier) never runs on silence — the dominant CPU/battery cost of always-on wake word
  detection — plus an adaptive noise-floor margin (AdaptiveNoiseGate, from :core:audio) so that gate
  keeps working in a sustained noisy environment instead of degrading to "always open" once ambient
  RMS sits above a fixed floor.
- WakeWordEngine.kt: forwards the RMS gate floor, adaptive-margin, and configurable audio source
  (AEC) constructor parameters through to AudioRecorder, and a comment noting the gate's invariant.
- audio/AudioProcessor.kt: logs a prediction score only above 0.05, so the log shows near misses
  rather than a line per inference, and formats it with Locale.US.

The pristine upstream source is kept as a git submodule at vendor/openwakeword-android-kt.

Each patch is maintained as a real, storable diff under core/wakeword/patches/ — one per patched
file, regenerated with scripts/regen_openwakeword_patch.sh whenever a patch changes. Which file a
patch covers is read out of the patch itself, so adding one to that folder is the whole of adding a
patch; nothing else lists them. scripts/verify_vendored_patches.sh asserts that src/main is exactly
upstream plus these patches, and .github/workflows/verify-vendor-patches.yml runs it whenever the
vendored tree or a patch changes — the 0.05 logging change above had been sitting in the vendored
copy unrecorded, which means the next re-vendor would have dropped it without failing a build. On an
upstream release, the scheduled sync-openwakeword.yml workflow re-vendors the sources and
auto-applies every patch file — opening a PR that's already merged + compiled + tested in the
common case. It only needs a manual merge if a patch genuinely conflicts (upstream changed the same
lines), in which case the PR says so explicitly with the reject details attached, for that patch
specifically — the other one still applies independently. scripts/check_openwakeword_version.sh
runs the same "would it still apply?" check locally, non-destructively.

